# Research Mission Control licensing

Copyright 2026 Dr. Christopher Pollin.

Research Mission Control uses two primary licenses with a file-level assignment:

- Code, tests, build tooling, configuration, and the complete installable
  Research Mission Control skill are licensed under the MIT License. The
  official text is in [LICENSES/MIT.txt](LICENSES/MIT.txt).
- Documentation, prompts, evaluation cases, release metadata, and the website
  are licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International
  License (CC BY 4.0). The official text is in
  [LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt](LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt).

`publication-manifest.json` defines the machine-checked path rules. The public
release builder rejects any distributed file that has no assignment or matches
more than one assignment. Files carrying third-party marks remain subject to
applicable trademark rights; the licenses do not grant trademark permission.

The synthetic demo application under demo/ assigns MIT to its HTML, CSS and JavaScript implementation and CC BY 4.0 to authored example data, source texts and documentation, as listed exactly in its export manifest and the publication manifest. Generated JavaScript files containing authored example data retain their CC BY 4.0 assignment. These examples contain no third-party portfolio data.

Third-party and brand assets retain their own terms. The local Sora and
Instrument Sans font files and their accompanying notices are licensed under
SIL Open Font License 1.1. The unchanged notices are distributed beside the
fonts in assets/fonts/. DHCraft marks, logos and favicons are proprietary,
identified as LicenseRef-DHCraft-Brand, with terms in
[LICENSES/DHCraft-Brand.txt](LICENSES/DHCraft-Brand.txt). Neither the project
licenses nor the example setup grant brand reuse rights.
